#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:1
msgid "Interoperability"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:3
msgid ""
"cuTile Rust can launch external CUDA kernels and borrow handles from other "
"libraries:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:6
msgid ""
"**Integrating external PTX or CUBIN kernels** — for CUDA C++ kernels, cuda-"
"oxide-generated PTX, or other CUDA module artifacts that you want to launch "
"alongside cuTile kernels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:7
msgid ""
"**Borrowing foreign CUDA handles** — wrap a `CUcontext` / `CUstream` from "
"another Rust binding crate (cudarc, Candle, hand-rolled FFI) so cuTile "
"kernels can run on handles you already own."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:8
msgid ""
"**Migrating from other tile DSLs** — conceptual mapping from Triton and "
"cuTile Python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:10
msgid ""
"External CUDA kernels participate in the same `DeviceOp` execution model as "
"tile kernels — sharing streams, chaining with `.then()`, and avoiding "
"unnecessary synchronization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:14
msgid "Integrating External PTX or CUBIN Kernels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:16
msgid "From CUDA C++"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:18
msgid ""
"Compile your CUDA C++ kernel to PTX (portable) or a `.cubin` (architecture-"
"specific):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:20
msgid ""
"# PTX — portable across GPU architectures, JIT-compiled at load time.\n"
"nvcc -ptx -arch=compute_80 my_kernel.cu -o my_kernel.ptx\n"
"\n"
"# cubin — pre-compiled for a single architecture, no JIT overhead.\n"
"nvcc -cubin -arch=sm_80 my_kernel.cu -o my_kernel.cubin\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:28
msgid ""
"**Architecture portability:** A `.cubin` file only runs on the exact SM "
"architecture it was compiled for. Code compiled with `-arch=sm_80` will not "
"load on an `sm_100` GPU. PTX avoids this problem — the CUDA driver JIT-"
"compiles it for the target GPU at load time, at the cost of a one-time "
"compilation delay. Prefer PTX unless you need to eliminate JIT overhead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:30
msgid "From cuda-oxide"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:32
msgid ""
"[cuda-oxide](https://nvidia.github.io/cuda-rust/cuda-oxide/) is CUDA Rust's "
"SIMT kernel programming model; it lives next to cuTile Rust in the [cuda-"
"rust repository](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuda-rust/tree/main/cuda-oxide). "
"Its documented output path is PTX: build or inspect the generated PTX with "
"`cargo oxide build` or `cargo oxide pipeline`, then load that PTX with "
"`load_module_from_ptx` and wrap the entry function in an `AsyncKernelLaunch` "
"or typed `DeviceOp`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:34
msgid ""
"If you need a CUBIN, compile the PTX through the normal CUDA toolchain as a "
"separate step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:36
msgid "Loading and Launching"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:38
msgid "Load the compiled module and get a handle to the entry function:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:40
msgid ""
"use cuda_async::device_context::{load_module_from_file, "
"load_module_from_ptx};\n"
"\n"
"// From cubin:\n"
"let module = load_module_from_file(\"my_kernel.cubin\", device_id)?;\n"
"\n"
"// Or from PTX (JIT-compiled at load time):\n"
"let ptx_src = include_str!(\"my_kernel.ptx\");\n"
"let module = load_module_from_ptx(ptx_src, device_id)?;\n"
"\n"
"let function = Arc::new(module.load_function(\"my_kernel_entry\")?);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:53
msgid ""
"Launch the kernel via `AsyncKernelLaunch`, which is a `DeviceOp` wrapping "
"the CUDA driver's kernel launch API:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:55
msgid ""
"use cuda_async::launch::AsyncKernelLaunch;\n"
"use cuda_core::LaunchConfig;\n"
"\n"
"let mut launcher = AsyncKernelLaunch::new(function.clone());\n"
"launcher.push_arg(num_elements as u32);\n"
"launcher.push_arg(scale);\n"
"// SAFETY: input and output are valid device allocations with at least\n"
"// num_elements f32 elements. output is exclusively written; input is\n"
"// read-only. Both remain allocated until this operation completes.\n"
"unsafe {\n"
"    launcher\n"
"        .push_device_ptr(input.device_pointer().cu_deviceptr())\n"
"        .push_device_ptr(output.device_pointer().cu_deviceptr());\n"
"}\n"
"launcher.set_launch_config(LaunchConfig {\n"
"    grid_dim: ((num_elements as u32 + 255) / 256, 1, 1),\n"
"    block_dim: (256, 1, 1),\n"
"    shared_mem_bytes: 0,\n"
"});\n"
"\n"
"// Execute as a DeviceOp — integrates with the async model.\n"
"launcher.await?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:80
msgid ""
"Scalar arguments implementing `DType` use the safe `push_arg` method. Device "
"pointers require `unsafe { push_device_ptr() }`. The caller must check that "
"allocations are valid on the target GPU, large enough for the kernel, and "
"free of conflicting accesses. Argument order and types must match the "
"kernel's signature. Rust and the CUDA driver cannot validate these "
"conditions; getting them wrong can cause undefined behavior or GPU faults."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:87
msgid ""
"To prevent data races, use stream ordering: operations chained with `."
"then()` on the same stream execute in order and see each other's writes. "
"Operations on different streams require explicit synchronization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:89
msgid ""
"**Why generated cuTile kernels don't require `unsafe`:** the `#[cutile::"
"entry]` macro generates launchers that call `push_device_ptr` internally, "
"but they do so safely because the framework controls both sides — device "
"pointers come from framework-managed allocations, and the ownership model "
"(`Partition` for exclusive access, `Arc<Tensor>` for shared reads) prevents "
"aliasing at the type level. Custom kernels bypass this: you are pushing "
"pointers the framework didn't allocate and can't track, so the safety burden "
"falls on you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:93
msgid "Wrapping as a Safe DeviceOp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:95
msgid ""
"Wrap a custom kernel launch in a struct that implements `DeviceOp`. The "
"struct's typed fields enforce the correct argument signature; `unsafe` is "
"confined to `execute`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:97
msgid ""
"use cuda_async::device_context::with_default_device_policy;\n"
"use cuda_async::device_future::DeviceFuture;\n"
"use cuda_async::device_operation::{DeviceOp, ExecutionContext};\n"
"use cuda_async::error::DeviceError;\n"
"use cuda_async::launch::AsyncKernelLaunch;\n"
"use cuda_async::scheduling_policies::SchedulingPolicy;\n"
"use cuda_core::{Function, LaunchConfig};\n"
"use std::future::IntoFuture;\n"
"\n"
"pub struct ScaleKernel {\n"
"    function: Arc<Function>,\n"
"    n: u32,\n"
"    scale: f32,\n"
"    input: Arc<Tensor<f32>>,\n"
"    output: Tensor<f32>,\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl DeviceOp for ScaleKernel {\n"
"    type Output = (Arc<Tensor<f32>>, Tensor<f32>);\n"
"\n"
"    // execute is unsafe because it enqueues async GPU work without\n"
"    // synchronizing — the returned tensors may still be in-flight.\n"
"    unsafe fn execute(\n"
"        self,\n"
"        ctx: &ExecutionContext,\n"
"    ) -> Result<<Self as DeviceOp>::Output, DeviceError> {\n"
"        let mut launcher = AsyncKernelLaunch::new(self.function);\n"
"        launcher.push_arg(self.n);\n"
"        launcher.push_arg(self.scale);\n"
"        // SAFETY: input and output are framework-managed Tensor "
"allocations.\n"
"        // input is shared (Arc, read-only); output is exclusively written.\n"
"        unsafe {\n"
"            launcher\n"
"                .push_device_ptr(self.input.device_pointer()."
"cu_deviceptr())\n"
"                .push_device_ptr(self.output.device_pointer()."
"cu_deviceptr());\n"
"        }\n"
"        launcher.set_launch_config(LaunchConfig {\n"
"            grid_dim: ((self.n + 255) / 256, 1, 1),\n"
"            block_dim: (256, 1, 1),\n"
"            shared_mem_bytes: 0,\n"
"        });\n"
"        unsafe { launcher.execute(ctx)? };\n"
"        Ok((self.input, self.output))\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"// IntoFuture is a supertrait of DeviceOp. Every custom DeviceOp\n"
"// needs this boilerplate to enable `.await` and `.sync()`.\n"
"impl IntoFuture for ScaleKernel {\n"
"    type Output = Result<(Arc<Tensor<f32>>, Tensor<f32>), DeviceError>;\n"
"    type IntoFuture = DeviceFuture<(Arc<Tensor<f32>>, Tensor<f32>), "
"ScaleKernel>;\n"
"    fn into_future(self) -> Self::IntoFuture {\n"
"        match with_default_device_policy(|policy| {\n"
"            let stream = policy.next_stream()?;\n"
"            Ok(DeviceFuture::scheduled(self, ExecutionContext::"
"new(stream)))\n"
"        }) {\n"
"            Ok(Ok(future)) => future,\n"
"            Ok(Err(e)) | Err(e) => DeviceFuture::failed(e),\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:161
msgid ""
"This is the same pattern the `#[cutile::entry]` macro uses to generate safe "
"launchers for tile kernels — no `unsafe` at the call site. Once wrapped, the "
"custom kernel composes with tile kernels through the usual `DeviceOp` "
"combinators. This pipeline runs a tile add (`z = x + y`), then the wrapped "
"scale kernel (`w = scale * z`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:163
msgid ""
"let (z_part, _x, _y) =\n"
"    tile_add::add(z.partition([tile_size]), x.clone(), y.clone()).await?;\n"
"let z: Tensor<f32> = z_part.unpartition();\n"
"\n"
"let w: Tensor<f32> = zeros(&[num_elements]).await?;\n"
"let (_z, w) = ScaleKernel {\n"
"    function: scale_function,\n"
"    n: num_elements as u32,\n"
"    scale,\n"
"    input: Arc::new(z),\n"
"    output: w,\n"
"}\n"
".await?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:179
msgid ""
"See [`interop.rs`](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuda-rust/blob/main/cutile-rs/"
"cutile-examples/examples/interop.rs) for a complete, runnable version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:183
msgid "Low-Level Driver Access"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:185
msgid ""
"For more direct control, use `with_context` to access the CUDA stream and "
"issue driver API calls directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:187
msgid ""
"use cuda_async::device_operation::{with_context, value, DeviceOp};\n"
"use cuda_async::device_operation::ExecutionContext;\n"
"use cuda_core::{malloc_async, memcpy_htod_async, free_async};\n"
"\n"
"let host_data: Vec<f32> = vec![1.0; num_elements];\n"
"let num_bytes = num_elements * std::mem::size_of::<f32>();\n"
"\n"
"// host_data is captured by reference — it must outlive the await so that\n"
"// the async memcpy can read from it until the stream synchronizes.\n"
"let op = with_context(|ctx: &ExecutionContext| {\n"
"    let stream = ctx.get_cuda_stream();\n"
"\n"
"    let dptr = unsafe {\n"
"        let dptr = malloc_async(num_bytes, stream).expect(\"device "
"allocation\");\n"
"        memcpy_htod_async(dptr, host_data.as_ptr(), num_elements, stream)\n"
"            .expect(\"host-to-device copy\");\n"
"        dptr\n"
"    };\n"
"\n"
"    value(dptr)\n"
"});\n"
"\n"
"let dptr = op.await?;\n"
"\n"
"// Clean up: free the device memory on a stream.\n"
"with_context(move |ctx: &ExecutionContext| {\n"
"    unsafe { free_async(dptr, ctx.get_cuda_stream()) }.expect(\"device "
"free\");\n"
"    value(())\n"
"})\n"
".await?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:220
msgid ""
"Driver calls inside the `unsafe` block must satisfy the CUDA API's safety "
"requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:225
msgid "Borrowing Foreign CUDA Handles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:227
msgid ""
"If your application already owns CUDA handles through another Rust binding "
"crate — cudarc, Candle, or a hand-rolled `bindgen` wrapper — wrap them into "
"a cuTile `Device`, `Stream`, `Module`, or `Function` without transferring "
"ownership."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:229
msgid ""
"The `borrow_raw` constructors take raw C primitives (`*mut c_void` for "
"opaque handles, `c_int` for `CUdevice`) rather than `cuda_bindings` "
"typedefs, so no nominal-type mismatch between binding crates gets in the way:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:231
msgid ""
"use core::ffi::{c_int, c_void};\n"
"use cuda_core::{Device, Stream};\n"
"\n"
"// `foreign` is a handle bundle from another Rust binding crate (cudarc,\n"
"// Candle, a bindgen wrapper, …). Its `CUcontext` / `CUstream` typedefs are\n"
"// nominally distinct from cuTile's, but the underlying C ABI is identical "
"—\n"
"// cast at the boundary and the handles flow through unchanged.\n"
"\n"
"// Safety: the caller guarantees the handles are valid, outlive the "
"returned\n"
"// wrappers, and are not concurrently destroyed.\n"
"let device = unsafe {\n"
"    Device::borrow_raw(\n"
"        foreign.cu_ctx as *mut c_void,\n"
"        foreign.cu_device as c_int,\n"
"        foreign.ordinal,\n"
"    )\n"
"};\n"
"let stream = unsafe { Stream::borrow_raw(foreign.cu_stream as *mut c_void, "
"&device) };\n"
"\n"
"// cuTile kernels now run on the borrowed stream.\n"
"let result = my_kernel(out, x).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:255
msgid ""
"Borrowed wrappers skip destruction on drop: `Stream` does not call "
"`cuStreamDestroy`, `Module` does not unload, and `Device` does not release "
"the primary context. The source framework retains full control over handle "
"lifetimes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/interoperability.md:257
msgid ""
"`Module::borrow_raw` and `Function::borrow_raw` follow the same pattern for "
"pre-compiled modules and already-resolved functions. See the "
"`cudarc_interop` example in `cutile-examples/examples/` for an end-to-end "
"walkthrough."
msgstr ""

